1
00:00:00,250 --> 00:00:01,414
This is Jupiter.

2
00:00:01,734 --> 00:00:04,645
You could fit more than thirteen hundred
Earths inside it.

3
00:00:04,965 --> 00:00:07,317
And in a few minutes, you're going to fall
into it.

4
00:00:07,637 --> 00:00:08,844
Not crash. Fall.

5
00:00:09,744 --> 00:00:13,988
Because Jupiter has no ground. No surface.
Nothing to land on.

6
00:00:14,308 --> 00:00:17,293
Just sky, getting thicker and hotter, all
the way down.

7
00:00:17,893 --> 00:00:20,399
We ran your fall through a physics
simulation,

8
00:00:20,399 --> 00:00:24,158
built on real measurements from the probe
that went there before you.

9
00:00:24,478 --> 00:00:27,122
Every number on the side of your screen
comes from it.

10
00:00:27,442 --> 00:00:29,497
And it showed us something we didn't
expect.

11
00:00:29,817 --> 00:00:33,812
At some point, the falling just stops. And
not because you hit anything.

12
00:00:39,312 --> 00:00:40,848
First, you have to get there.

13
00:00:41,168 --> 00:00:45,181
Jupiter is wrapped in the most intense
radiation belts of any planet.

14
00:00:45,501 --> 00:00:48,547
Without heavy shielding, they would kill
you within hours.

15
00:00:48,867 --> 00:00:53,332
NASA's Juno spacecraft keeps its
electronics inside a titanium vault,

16
00:00:53,332 --> 00:00:55,661
just to survive flying through them.

17
00:00:56,261 --> 00:00:57,487
Then there's the speed.

18
00:00:57,807 --> 00:01:01,658
Jupiter's gravity is so strong that
anything falling in from

19
00:01:01,658 --> 00:01:05,574
deep space hits the air at about sixty
kilometres per second.

20
00:01:05,894 --> 00:01:09,443
Fast enough to cross the Atlantic in about
a minute and a half.

21
00:01:10,043 --> 00:01:13,562
In nineteen ninety-five, a NASA probe did
exactly that.

22
00:01:13,882 --> 00:01:17,236
The Galileo probe hit the atmosphere so
hard that the gas

23
00:01:17,236 --> 00:01:20,589
in front of it became hotter than the
surface of the Sun.

24
00:01:20,909 --> 00:01:24,591
In about two minutes, most of the front of
its heat shield burned away.

25
00:01:25,191 --> 00:01:29,769
You won't do that. Your ship fires its
engines and comes to a complete stop,

26
00:01:29,769 --> 00:01:31,094
just above the clouds.

27
00:01:31,414 --> 00:01:35,963
Twelve tonnes, four metres wide, built
like the strongest submarines on Earth.

28
00:01:36,283 --> 00:01:38,994
From here, the rules are simple. Engines
off.

29
00:01:39,314 --> 00:01:40,041
You fall.

30
00:01:41,141 --> 00:01:43,106
The first thing you'd notice is your
weight.

31
00:01:43,426 --> 00:01:46,340
Jupiter's gravity is two and a half times
Earth's.

32
00:01:46,340 --> 00:01:50,828
Just lifting your arm would feel like
lifting it underwater, with weights on.

33
00:01:51,148 --> 00:01:54,870
Below you, a floor of white cloud
stretches past every horizon.

34
00:01:55,190 --> 00:01:58,502
It's ammonia ice, colder than minus one
hundred degrees.

35
00:01:59,102 --> 00:02:00,178
And it's all moving.

36
00:02:00,498 --> 00:02:04,329
The winds up here blow at over five
hundred kilometres per hour,

37
00:02:04,329 --> 00:02:06,904
in bands that wrap around the whole
planet.

38
00:02:07,224 --> 00:02:10,982
One storm, the Great Red Spot, is wider
than the entire Earth.

39
00:02:11,582 --> 00:02:13,168
You drop into the white clouds.

40
00:02:13,488 --> 00:02:16,766
The air up here is thin, so almost nothing
slows you down.

41
00:02:17,086 --> 00:02:21,404
In forty seconds, you're falling at more
than two thousand kilometres per hour.

42
00:02:22,004 --> 00:02:25,700
Under the white is a second layer of
cloud. Brown, and orange.

43
00:02:26,020 --> 00:02:29,140
It's ammonium hydrosulfide. If you opened
a window,

44
00:02:29,140 --> 00:02:32,259
it would smell like rotten eggs and
cleaning fluid.

45
00:02:32,579 --> 00:02:36,689
Chemicals like this, broken apart by
sunlight, are thought to paint

46
00:02:36,689 --> 00:02:40,553
Jupiter's stripes. Even now, nobody is
sure exactly which ones.

47
00:02:41,153 --> 00:02:42,620
Two and a half minutes in,

48
00:02:42,620 --> 00:02:45,383
fifty kilometres down, you reach the third
layer.

49
00:02:45,703 --> 00:02:48,747
Water clouds. Real ones, like the clouds
on Earth.

50
00:02:49,067 --> 00:02:51,301
And for a moment, something strange
happens.

51
00:02:51,621 --> 00:02:56,217
The temperature outside your window climbs
past twenty degrees. A warm spring day.

52
00:02:56,537 --> 00:02:59,330
The pressure is about seven times Earth's
air.

53
00:02:59,330 --> 00:03:01,941
The same as diving sixty metres
underwater.

54
00:03:02,541 --> 00:03:05,366
But these are the clouds where Jupiter's
storms are born.

55
00:03:05,686 --> 00:03:08,066
Its thunderstorms are strong enough to
fling

56
00:03:08,066 --> 00:03:10,663
ice more than twenty kilometres up into
the sky.

57
00:03:10,983 --> 00:03:14,609
Up there, the ice meets ammonia gas and
melts into slush.

58
00:03:14,609 --> 00:03:17,344
Scientists call these hailstones
mushballs.

59
00:03:17,664 --> 00:03:19,914
And the storms crackle with lightning,

60
00:03:19,914 --> 00:03:23,586
seen flickering on Jupiter's night side by
passing spacecraft.

61
00:03:24,186 --> 00:03:27,434
Below the water clouds, the last of the
sunlight is gone.

62
00:03:27,754 --> 00:03:29,808
From here on, it's completely dark.

63
00:03:30,128 --> 00:03:33,404
The only light is the lightning, flashing
somewhere around you.

64
00:03:34,404 --> 00:03:37,635
Five and a half minutes in, a hundred
kilometres down,

65
00:03:37,635 --> 00:03:40,327
the air outside is hotter than boiling
water.

66
00:03:40,647 --> 00:03:43,891
You're still falling at more than eight
hundred kilometres per hour.

67
00:03:44,211 --> 00:03:47,455
But the air is getting thicker, and it's
starting to hold you back.

68
00:03:48,055 --> 00:03:50,344
At seven minutes, you pass a milestone.

69
00:03:50,664 --> 00:03:52,944
This is where the Galileo probe went
silent.

70
00:03:53,264 --> 00:03:56,143
It drifted down on a parachute for almost
an hour,

71
00:03:56,143 --> 00:03:60,000
sending back everything it measured, until
the heat overwhelmed it.

72
00:04:00,320 --> 00:04:04,270
Twenty-two times Earth's air pressure. A
hundred and fifty degrees.

73
00:04:04,590 --> 00:04:07,744
Nothing we've ever built has been deeper
inside Jupiter.

74
00:04:08,064 --> 00:04:12,408
Everything from here on, nobody has ever
measured. It comes from physics.

75
00:04:13,008 --> 00:04:15,913
Half an hour in. Three hundred kilometres
down.

76
00:04:16,233 --> 00:04:19,242
The temperature outside passes five
hundred degrees.

77
00:04:19,562 --> 00:04:22,277
And then something happens that you'd
never expect,

78
00:04:22,277 --> 00:04:24,140
at the bottom of a pitch-black sky.

79
00:04:24,460 --> 00:04:25,957
The darkness starts to glow.

80
00:04:26,557 --> 00:04:29,192
Anything this hot gives off its own light.

81
00:04:29,192 --> 00:04:32,142
A dull red, like the ring of an electric
stove.

82
00:04:32,462 --> 00:04:36,243
It's the air itself. Above you, below you,
in every direction.

83
00:04:36,563 --> 00:04:40,256
And the deeper you go, the brighter it
gets. Red, then orange.

84
00:04:40,856 --> 00:04:42,848
Meanwhile, the pressure keeps climbing.

85
00:04:43,168 --> 00:04:46,018
Two hundred times Earth's air. Then three
hundred.

86
00:04:46,338 --> 00:04:48,606
Every square centimetre of your hull is

87
00:04:48,606 --> 00:04:51,106
now holding up the weight of a grand
piano.

88
00:04:51,706 --> 00:04:52,988
An hour and a half in.

89
00:04:53,308 --> 00:04:56,400
You're almost six hundred kilometres down.
Outside,

90
00:04:56,400 --> 00:04:59,370
it's over a thousand degrees, and glowing
orange.

91
00:04:59,690 --> 00:05:02,688
And the pressure reaches eleven hundred
times Earth's air.

92
00:05:03,008 --> 00:05:06,101
That's the pressure at the bottom of the
Mariana Trench,

93
00:05:06,101 --> 00:05:08,089
the deepest point in Earth's oceans.

94
00:05:08,689 --> 00:05:11,621
It's the limit of the strongest submarines
ever built.

95
00:05:11,941 --> 00:05:15,886
Every square centimetre of your hull is
now holding up a small car.

96
00:05:16,206 --> 00:05:20,342
And right here, ninety-eight minutes after
you let go, your ship gives way.

97
00:05:21,942 --> 00:05:23,806
Here's what puts that in perspective.

98
00:05:24,126 --> 00:05:26,494
You fell almost six hundred kilometres.

99
00:05:26,494 --> 00:05:29,167
Jupiter is seventy thousand kilometres
deep.

100
00:05:29,487 --> 00:05:32,323
You didn't even make it one percent of the
way to the centre.

101
00:05:32,923 --> 00:05:33,900
So let's cheat.

102
00:05:34,220 --> 00:05:38,174
Imagine your ship can't be crushed, and
can't melt. It just keeps falling.

103
00:05:38,494 --> 00:05:39,738
How far does it get?

104
00:05:40,058 --> 00:05:42,288
This is where the simulation surprised us.

105
00:05:42,888 --> 00:05:45,034
The air around you is now so dense,

106
00:05:45,034 --> 00:05:49,266
it slows you to the speed of a car on a
motorway. Then a car in town.

107
00:05:49,586 --> 00:05:51,411
Hours pass. Then days.

108
00:05:51,731 --> 00:05:54,613
Two thousand kilometres down. Three
thousand.

109
00:05:54,933 --> 00:05:57,851
The hydrogen around you is thousands of
degrees,

110
00:05:57,851 --> 00:06:00,890
and squeezed so hard it stops behaving
like a gas.

111
00:06:01,490 --> 00:06:04,140
There's no moment where you splash into an
ocean.

112
00:06:04,460 --> 00:06:08,933
The sky just gets thicker, and thicker,
until it flows around you like a liquid.

113
00:06:09,253 --> 00:06:13,269
And somewhere around five thousand
kilometres down, your ship stops.

114
00:06:13,589 --> 00:06:15,109
Not because it hit anything.

115
00:06:15,429 --> 00:06:19,385
Because the hydrogen around it is now as
dense as the ship itself.

116
00:06:19,985 --> 00:06:20,781
You float.

117
00:06:21,101 --> 00:06:26,179
Six and a half days after you let go, five
thousand three hundred kilometres down,

118
00:06:26,179 --> 00:06:29,152
in a glowing ocean with no surface and no
floor.

119
00:06:29,472 --> 00:06:31,557
Less than a tenth of the way to the
centre.

120
00:06:31,877 --> 00:06:34,828
Jupiter doesn't swallow you. It just won't
let you sink.

121
00:06:36,228 --> 00:06:37,888
But we can keep going without you.

122
00:06:38,208 --> 00:06:40,353
Around ten thousand kilometres down,

123
00:06:40,353 --> 00:06:43,212
the pressure passes a million times
Earth's air.

124
00:06:43,532 --> 00:06:46,501
And hydrogen, the simplest element in the
universe,

125
00:06:46,501 --> 00:06:49,936
turns into something that doesn't exist
naturally on Earth.

126
00:06:50,256 --> 00:06:51,212
A liquid metal.

127
00:06:51,812 --> 00:06:55,924
Squeezed this hard, the electrons break
free of their atoms and flow,

128
00:06:55,924 --> 00:06:57,711
like they do in a copper wire.

129
00:06:58,031 --> 00:07:02,557
This ocean of metal swirls as Jupiter
spins once every ten hours.

130
00:07:02,877 --> 00:07:06,404
And it generates the strongest magnetic
field of any planet.

131
00:07:07,004 --> 00:07:09,616
If you could see that magnetic field from
Earth,

132
00:07:09,616 --> 00:07:12,011
it would look about as big as the full
Moon.

133
00:07:12,331 --> 00:07:14,993
From more than six hundred million
kilometres away.

134
00:07:15,593 --> 00:07:19,194
Some scientists have even suggested that
carbon from lightning

135
00:07:19,194 --> 00:07:23,144
could be squeezed into diamonds, and sink
through Jupiter like hail.

136
00:07:23,464 --> 00:07:25,133
Nobody knows yet if it's true.

137
00:07:25,733 --> 00:07:27,786
And at the very centre, there's a core.

138
00:07:28,106 --> 00:07:32,317
Scientists expected a solid ball. Juno
found something stranger.

139
00:07:32,317 --> 00:07:37,120
A fuzzy core, partly dissolved, spread
through a huge part of the planet.

140
00:07:37,440 --> 00:07:39,169
Around twenty thousand degrees.

141
00:07:39,169 --> 00:07:42,348
More than three times hotter than the
surface of the Sun.

142
00:07:42,668 --> 00:07:45,392
Under a pressure tens of millions of times
Earth's air.

143
00:07:45,992 --> 00:07:47,060
So that's the fall.

144
00:07:47,380 --> 00:07:50,398
Seven minutes to go deeper than anything
we've ever sent.

145
00:07:50,718 --> 00:07:53,792
Ninety-eight minutes to crush the
strongest ship we could build.

146
00:07:54,112 --> 00:07:57,082
And six and a half days to come to rest,
floating,

147
00:07:57,082 --> 00:08:00,765
five thousand kilometres down, without
ever touching a bottom.

148
00:08:01,365 --> 00:08:04,186
Jupiter is made of almost the same stuff
as the Sun.

149
00:08:04,506 --> 00:08:08,278
It would need about eighty times more mass
to start shining on its own.

150
00:08:08,598 --> 00:08:12,942
Instead, it's the biggest thing in the
Solar System that never became a star.

151
00:08:13,542 --> 00:08:15,909
Next time, we leave the planets behind.

152
00:08:16,409 --> 00:08:18,670
Because some planets don't have a star at
all.

153
00:08:18,670 --> 00:08:20,684
And one of them could be heading our way.

154
00:08:22,284 --> 00:08:24,171
Subscribe, and fall with us again.

